<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="S8.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.1 </span>Negative Log-likelihood (NLL)</h3>

<div id="S8.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Imagine that <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is a probability distribution function. Here, <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> can be anything, it could be a number, or a vector, or some other irregular-dimensional object. If <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> is continuous, what this means is that <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\cdot p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\cdot p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability that a random sample drawn from <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> will be in the <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> neighborhood of <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> for vanishingly small <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and our sums below will become integration. Now let’s write down the probability of a sample <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},\ldots,x_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},\ldots,x_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> observations. We will make the simplifying assumption that each sample drawn from the probability distribution is independent of another:</p>
<table id="S8.EGx34" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(\boldsymbol{x}|p)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}p(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(\boldsymbol{x}|p)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}p(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Now, the way real world is set up is that we often don’t know the true distribution <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. What we do have are the observations <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, and what we are really trying to do is generalize the nature of the world in some way that we can make useful predictions about it by learning from these observations <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math>. If we could figure out <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, we’d be set. So in summary:</p>
<table id="S8.EGx35" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S8.Ex44.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">What we have:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{x}\text{, the observations from real world}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>, the observations from real world</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{x}\text{, the observations from real world}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex45"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S8.Ex45.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">What we want:</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\text{, the underlying probability distribution that generated the observations}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>, the underlying probability distribution that generated the observations</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\text{, the underlying probability distribution that generated the observations}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Since the real world is infinitely complex, this is a hopeless pursuit. But, can we recover perhaps a simpler model of the world that may be useful in making some relevant predictions? Let’s call this distribution <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and basically <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is something we will iterate on and try to get as close to <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> as possible. If <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> were the real state of the world, the probability of <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> under <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> would be:</p>
<table id="S8.EGx36" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex46"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(\boldsymbol{x}|q)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}q(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(\boldsymbol{x}|q)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}q(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">We could now iterate over <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to maximize this probability and we would be aligning our assumed model of the world to what we have really observed and hopefully <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> will be a decent approximation of <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, since we are iterating over <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, we could also interpret this probability as “Likelihood of <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> being the true state of the world given that <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> is what we have observed in reality”. This allows us to see world as a fixed reference point and change <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to align with reality. This is often written as</p>
<table id="S8.EGx37" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex47"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(q|\boldsymbol{x})=P(\boldsymbol{x}|q)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}q(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(q|\boldsymbol{x})=P(\boldsymbol{x}|q)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}q(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">and the math doesn’t change at all, this is just a matter of interpretation. One practical consideration is that these probabilities can be small, so multiplying them often leads to extremely small numbers (e.g. if an LLM vocabulary is 32k then probability of each token can be <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{32000}=3.13\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>32000</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>3.13</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{32000}=3.13\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> and if you have a sentence with 10 tokens the multiplying these 10 times puts you around <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-50}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-50}</annotation></semantics></math>.). To make computations manageable we can take a log, which also has the nice property that our product will turn into a sum (e.g. <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log{\left(\frac{1}{32000}\right)}=-10.37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>32000</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>10.37</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log{\left(\frac{1}{32000}\right)}=-10.37</annotation></semantics></math> on the other hand, and much easier to manage - base here is <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>). Since log is a strictly monotone increasing function, we can maximize the log of probability instead of probability and it would amount to the same thing. Now, since probability is always less than 1, the log values will always be negative. What if we multiplied everything with a negative symbol to turn all the log values into positive numbers, with a wonderful side-effect that the maximization problem now turns into a minimization problem. So what we are really trying to do while finding a good <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is minimize the negative log-likelihood (NLL):</p>
<table id="S8.EGx38" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex48"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex48.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Minimize: NLL}(q|\boldsymbol{x})=-log\left(\mathcal{L}(q|\boldsymbol{x})\right)=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}log\left(q(x_{i})\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Minimize: NLL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Minimize: NLL}(q|\boldsymbol{x})=-log\left(\mathcal{L}(q|\boldsymbol{x})\right)=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}log\left(q(x_{i})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.6" class="ltx_p">The goal is to get the NLL of <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> down as close as possible to the NLL of true distribution, <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. We must keep the specification of <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently general, otherwise we risk overfitting to the observed sample while driving down the NLL. Likelihood was a concept introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fisher (1922)</a></cite>.</p>
<blockquote id="S8.SS1.p1.7" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S8.SS1.p1.7.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Low NLL <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\simeq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\simeq</annotation></semantics></math> High Likelihood <math id="S8.SS1.p1.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\simeq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\simeq</annotation></semantics></math> High probability</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.2 </span>Entropy</h3>

<div id="S8.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Entropy is sort of how much “randomness” is in a system. So for example, with the distribution <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> that the world actually follows (we don’t know <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, of course, but we assume it exists), we wrote down the probability of sample <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}</annotation></semantics></math> earlier. What’s a reasonable definition of randomness when it comes to a probability distribution? What we are trying to capture is how deterministic is the realized value of a random variable. If the realized value is highly deterministic, let’s say the random variable always take a fixed value of 1, then entropy should be zero. In this case the entire probability mass is centered on a single number. On the other hand, if it can take two values, say 0 and 1 with equal probability, then there is more entropy, but it is still contained. Each of 0 and 1 has a 0.5 probability of occurring. If we rolled a dice, then each number would have a probability of <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/6</annotation></semantics></math> occurring, and a dice has more randomness. If we have many values, each one with a low probability, then the entropy is high. The lower this probability, the higher the entropy. To define entropy we could just take negative log likelihood of this probability. So in the first case that would be <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log(1)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log(1)=0</annotation></semantics></math>, in the second case it would be <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log(1/2)=0.69" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.69</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log(1/2)=0.69</annotation></semantics></math> and in the case of the dice it would be <math id="S8.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\log(1/6)=1.79" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.79</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\log(1/6)=1.79</annotation></semantics></math>. So we are getting somewhere. We have a way of defining entropy at least for these simple cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">But not all distributions are uniform in the sense that each value a random variable can take does not occur with same probability. What if we have a loaded dice where probability of some numbers was higher than others? So what we want to do in the general case is take expectation of this NLL over all the values the random variable can take.</p>
<table id="S8.EGx39" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex49"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S8.Ex49.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Entropy</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex49.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{Expected value of}-log(x)\text{ over all }x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Expected value of</mtext><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> over all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{Expected value of}-log(x)\text{ over all }x</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex50"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex50.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{x}p(x)log(p(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>x</mi></munder></mstyle><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{x}p(x)log(p(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Where the sum is over all possible <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. So, entropy is simply the expected value of NLL of the true distribution of the system. If the entropy is close to zero (and therefore the NLL is), you know <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> fairly deterministically. In practice, what this means is that if you draw a sample and don’t see much randomness (e.g. the sun has been rising to the east for several millennia) you can be reasonably sure of the underlying distribution <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. the sun should rise to the east tomorrow). On the other hand if the likelihood is low, you don’t really have confidence in your <math id="S8.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The concept of entropy originated in thermodynamics, where it was first introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clausius (1865)</a></cite> as a measure of energy dispersal and process irreversibility. This macroscopic view was later given a microscopic, statistical foundation by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boltzmann (1877)</a></cite>, who related entropy to the number of possible microstates of a system. The concept found a powerful new application in the 20th century when <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shannon (1948)</a></cite> independently developed a parallel formulation for information entropy, quantifying uncertainty and information content in communication.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="S8.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S8.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Low NLL <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\simeq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\simeq</annotation></semantics></math> High Likelihood <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\simeq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\simeq</annotation></semantics></math> High probability <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\simeq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\simeq</annotation></semantics></math> High certainty <math id="S8.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\simeq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≃</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\simeq</annotation></semantics></math> Low randomness</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.3 </span>Cross entropy</h3>

<div id="S8.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, we don’t really know <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, the true distribution. What we are trying to do is calculate the NLL of <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and then minimize that to get as close to <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> as possible. And in doing so, what we are doing is calculating the NLL using samples from the real world. These samples are drawn from the true distribution of the real world - <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. As such, if we were to calculate the expected value of the NLL that we are trying to optimize, that would be cross entropy, and would come out to be something like this:</p>
<table id="S8.EGx40" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex51"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S8.Ex51.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">Cross entropy</span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex51.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{Expected value of NLL$(q|x)$ when real distribution is $p$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Expected value of NLL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext> when real distribution is </mtext><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{Expected value of NLL$(q|x)$ when real distribution is $p$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex52"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex52.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\sum_{x}p(x)log(q(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>x</mi></munder></mstyle><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\sum_{x}p(x)log(q(x))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">So basically, what entropy is measuring is expected NLL of true distribution when we draw a sample. What cross entropy is measuring is expected NLL of our approximate distribution when we draw a sample. Cross entropy can never be less than entropy, a fact that follows from our comment about NLL above but which we will not prove here. What you really want is to minimize the cross entropy value to get as close as possible to the entropy so as to get <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> close to the true distribution <math id="S8.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">But, you cannot really calculate cross entropy since the true distribution <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> isn’t known. So you can only approximate it with the observed samples. For example, if you got a sample <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> then you can assume that they have probability <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x_{i})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x_{i})=1</annotation></semantics></math> under true distribution (since that is what we observed) and calculate cross entropy which is, of course the same thing as NLL of <math id="S8.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> (since we got to cross entropy by taking expectation of NLL in the first place).</p>
<table id="S8.EGx41" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex53"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex53.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Minimize: Cross entropy loss = }-\sum_{i=1}^{n}log\left(q(x_{i})\right)=-log\left(\mathcal{L}(q|\boldsymbol{x})\right)=\text{NLL}(q|\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Minimize: Cross entropy loss = </mtext><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>NLL</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Minimize: Cross entropy loss = }-\sum_{i=1}^{n}log\left(q(x_{i})\right)=-log\left(\mathcal{L}(q|\boldsymbol{x})\right)=\text{NLL}(q|\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As such, when we use cross entropy loss what we are really using is the NLL of our model <math id="S8.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and as such the term negative log-likelihood more accurately describes the cross entropy loss. The concept of cross-entropy originates from the foundational principles of information entropy introduced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shannon (1948)</a></cite> and was more formally derived from the related work on relative entropy by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kullback and Leibler (1951)</a></cite>. Its modern application as a pivotal loss function in machine learning is anchored by work on neural network training and model distillation, as exemplified by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hinton et al. (2015)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.4 </span>KL Divergence</h3>

<div id="S8.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Now that we understand entropy and cross entropy, KL divergence, due to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kullback and Leibler (1951)</a></cite>, is fairly straightforward to understand. It is simply the difference between cross entropy and entropy:</p>
<table id="S8.EGx42" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex54"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><span id="S8.Ex54.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">KL Divergence <math id="S8.Ex54.m1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(p\|q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p\|q)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex54.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\text{Cross entropy - entropy}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mtext>Cross entropy - entropy</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\text{Cross entropy - entropy}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex55"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex55.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{KL}(p\|q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{KL}(p\|q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex55.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\left(-\sum_{x}p(x)log(q(x))\right)-\left(-\sum_{x}p(x)log(p(x))\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>x</mi></munder></mstyle><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>x</mi></munder></mstyle><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>l</mi><mi>o</mi><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left(-\sum_{x}p(x)log(q(x))\right)-\left(-\sum_{x}p(x)log(p(x))\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex56"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex56.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{KL}(p\|q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{KL}(p\|q)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex56.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{x}p(x)log\left(\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>x</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{x}p(x)log\left(\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">What KL divergence measures is how much expected NLL is higher under the approximate distribution <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> compared to the true distribution <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. In essence, KL divergence is a measure of how much extra we are paying (in terms of NLL) by assuming the world behaves like <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> when the true distribution is <math id="S8.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. Since entropy of the system is fixed, minimizing cross entropy is the same thing as minimizing KL divergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">KL divergence is a measure of how far a distribution <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is from the real distribution <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>. If we look at the terms we can see that KL divergence will heavily penalize situations where the distribution <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> assigns a very low probability to common events on the real distribution <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (since <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(x)</annotation></semantics></math> will go to zero, making the term go to infinity). This can cause <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> to distribute probability across the possibilities and explore more. KL divergence does not penalize nearly as much if <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> also assigns some probability to parts of distribution that are highly unlikely in the true distribution <math id="S8.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> since the term simply goes to zero.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In certain situations, you find yourself sampling from distribution <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> rather than <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and you still would like to have a measure of distance of the two distributions <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. In such cases, you use KL divergence with the roles of <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> flipped such that</p>
<table id="S8.EGx43" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex57"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex57.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle D_{KL}(q\|p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle D_{KL}(q\|p)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex57.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{x}q(x)log\left(\frac{q(x)}{p(x)}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>x</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{x}q(x)log\left(\frac{q(x)}{p(x)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This is sometimes also called reverse KL divergence (i.e. reverse of <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{KL}(p\|q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{KL}(p\|q)</annotation></semantics></math>). The effect of this is that the formulation penalizes <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> heavily for assigning probability to events that are highly unlikely under <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> (since <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> goes to zero). This has the opposite effect of making <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> focus on known areas in <math id="S8.SS4.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.5 </span>Importance Sampling</h3>

<div id="S8.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the situation where you are interested in estimating the average height of a person at a basketball event. The event consists of about 5% of pro basketball players and 95% people from the general population. You are able to get the height of about 200 randomly selected pro-players at the event, from publicly available data about them (let’s say <math id="S8.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},...x_{200}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>200</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},...x_{200}</annotation></semantics></math>). You are also able to get the height of about 100 randomly identified participants at the event (let’s say <math id="S8.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{201},...,x_{300}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>201</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>300</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{201},...,x_{300}</annotation></semantics></math>). How do you calculate the average height? You know that taking a simple average of the 200 numbers is going to be wrong, since there are far fewer pro basketball players at the event than the general population, you know that pro basketball players are taller as a rule, and you know that only 5% of the people at the event are pro players. Assigning those 100 pro-player samples a weight of 50% (as would happen if you were to simply average all samples) would be wrong. What is the simplest thing you can do?</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A reasonable thing to do would be to weight the samples based on how much of that is present in the full event population. We know the average height of pro-players is average of <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1},...,x_{200}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>200</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1},...,x_{200}</annotation></semantics></math> and the average height of everyone else is average of <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{201},...,x_{300}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>201</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>300</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{201},...,x_{300}</annotation></semantics></math>. A good estimate of the overall average would be to weight the average of the first sample at 5% and the second one at 95% and so:</p>
<table id="S8.EGx44" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex58"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex58.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Estimate" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Estimate</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex58.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0.05\left(\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}+...+x_{200}}{200}\right)+0.95\left(\frac{x_{201}+x_{202}+...+x_{300}}{100}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>200</mn></msub></mrow><mn>200</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.95</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>201</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>202</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>300</mn></msub></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0.05\left(\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}+...+x_{200}}{200}\right)+0.95\left(\frac{x_{201}+x_{202}+...+x_{300}}{100}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS5.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Let’s formalize this a little bit. Let <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be the original distribution of pro-players and non-players in the conference and since we have 5% pro players: <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(pp)=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(pp)=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(np)=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(np)=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>. Let’s also use <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the distribution which we used to sample. Since we have 200 pp and 100 np the probability distribution that <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> follows is <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(pp)=\frac{2}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(pp)=\frac{2}{3}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(np)=\frac{1}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(np)=\frac{1}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let’s try to rewrite the above formula in terms of <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S8.EGx45" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex59"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex59.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Estimate" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Estimate</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex59.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=0.05\left(\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}+...+x_{200}}{200}\right)+0.95\left(\frac{x_{201}+x_{202}+...+x_{300}}{100}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>200</mn></msub></mrow><mn>200</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.95</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>201</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>202</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>300</mn></msub></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=0.05\left(\frac{x_{1}+x_{2}+...+x_{200}}{200}\right)+0.95\left(\frac{x_{201}+x_{202}+...+x_{300}}{100}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex60"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex60.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{0.05}{200}\sum_{i=1}^{200}x_{i}+\frac{0.95}{100}\sum_{i=201}^{300}x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>0.05</mn><mn>200</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>200</mn></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>0.95</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>201</mn></mrow><mn>300</mn></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{0.05}{200}\sum_{i=1}^{200}x_{i}+\frac{0.95}{100}\sum_{i=201}^{300}x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex61"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex61.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{300}\left[\frac{0.05}{\frac{2}{3}}\sum_{i=1}^{200}x_{i}+\frac{0.95}{\frac{1}{3}}\sum_{i=201}^{300}x_{i}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>300</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>0.05</mn><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>200</mn></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>0.95</mn><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>201</mn></mrow><mn>300</mn></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{300}\left[\frac{0.05}{\frac{2}{3}}\sum_{i=1}^{200}x_{i}+\frac{0.95}{\frac{1}{3}}\sum_{i=201}^{300}x_{i}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex62"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex62.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{300}\left[\frac{P(pp)}{Q(pp)}\sum_{i=1}^{200}x_{i}+\frac{P(np)}{Q(np)}\sum_{i=201}^{300}x_{i}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>300</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>200</mn></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>201</mn></mrow><mn>300</mn></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{300}\left[\frac{P(pp)}{Q(pp)}\sum_{i=1}^{200}x_{i}+\frac{P(np)}{Q(np)}\sum_{i=201}^{300}x_{i}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S8.Ex63"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex63.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{300}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{200}\frac{P(pp)}{Q(pp)}x_{i}+\sum_{i=201}^{300}\frac{P(np)}{Q(np)}x_{i}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>300</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>200</mn></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>201</mn></mrow><mn>300</mn></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{300}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{200}\frac{P(pp)}{Q(pp)}x_{i}+\sum_{i=201}^{300}\frac{P(np)}{Q(np)}x_{i}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS5.p2.3" class="ltx_p">This is the core idea of importance sampling where you sample from a distribution <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> (either because it is more convenient or <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is hard to sample from) and you can get an estimate at population level by weighing the samples appropriately by the ratio of the two distributions. Let’s generalize this further. Let’s assume that we have <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> observations total sampled from the population following the distribution <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the probability of <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> being sampled, and <math id="S8.SS5.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is the true probability. Then we can write the above formula as:</p>
<table id="S8.EGx46" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Estimate" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Estimate</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.E18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{P(x_{i})}{Q(x_{i})}x_{i}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{N}\frac{P(x_{i})}{Q(x_{i})}x_{i}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S8.SS5.p2.4" class="ltx_p">This is importance sampling. The concept of importance sampling as a variance reduction technique for Monte Carlo methods has its roots in the late 1940s and early 1950s, primarily driven by research in statistical physics. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahn (1949)</a></cite> laid the foundational groundwork for the method, demonstrating how to use a biased sampling distribution to more efficiently estimate quantities of interest in neutron transport problems. This idea was further formalized and popularized in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kahn and Harris (1951)</a></cite>, which provided a more rigorous mathematical framework and showcased the significant efficiency improvements achievable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.6 </span>Critique system prompt for code style</h3>

<div id="S8.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">You are a highly experienced and meticulous code style critic. Your role is to analyze a given code block and provide a detailed, objective critique of its stylistic qualities depending on the programming language of the code:</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Your Persona:</span></p>
<ul id="S8.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Knowledgeable:</em> You have a deep understanding of code conventions, design principles, and best practices across many programming languages (e.g., Python’s PEP 8, JavaScript’s Airbnb style guide, etc.).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Objective:</em> Your critique is based on established principles, not personal preference. You will cite the reasoning behind your suggestions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Helpful:</em> Your goal is to guide the user to write more readable, maintainable, and professional code. Your tone should be constructive and encouraging.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Core Task:</span>
Analyze the user-provided code block and perform a critique based on the following criteria.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Critique Criteria:</span></p>
<ol id="S8.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S8.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Readability &amp; Formatting:</em></p>
<ul id="S8.I2.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I2.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is the code well-formatted and easy to read?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does it use consistent indentation, spacing, and line breaks?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are complex lines of code broken down logically?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Naming Conventions:</em></p>
<ul id="S8.I2.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are variable, function, and class names descriptive and meaningful?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does the code consistently follow a single casing style (e.g., <span id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">snake_case</span>, <span id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">camelCase</span>, <span id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PascalCase</span>)?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are acronyms and abbreviations handled consistently?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Comments &amp; Documentation:</em></p>
<ul id="S8.I2.i3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are comments used effectively to explain <em id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">why</em> the code does something, not just <em id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what</em> it does?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are complex functions or classes accompanied by appropriate documentation (e.g., docstrings)?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are comments formatted consistently?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Modularity &amp; Structure:</em></p>
<ul id="S8.I2.i4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I2.i4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is the code broken down into logical, single-purpose functions or methods?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are functions or methods excessively long?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Are code blocks well-organized and clearly separated?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I2.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Robustness &amp; Best Practices:</em></p>
<ul id="S8.I2.i5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does the code handle potential errors gracefully (e.g., no bare <span id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">except</span> blocks)?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does it avoid hardcoding “magic numbers” or string literals?</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I2.i5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Is resource management handled properly (e.g., closing files, releasing connections)?</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output Format:</span>
Your response must be structured clearly:</p>
<ul id="S8.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Summary:</em> Start with a brief, high-level summary of the code’s overall style.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Detailed Critique:</em> For each of the five criteria listed above, provide a specific critique.</p>
<ul id="S8.I3.i2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Clearly state which criterion you are addressing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each point of critique, quote a specific line or block of code from the user’s input to illustrate the issue.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Explain the problem with that specific code snippet.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">–</span></span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Provide a concrete, improved example showing how to fix the issue.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S8.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Overall Recommendation:</em> Conclude with a final paragraph that summarizes your key recommendations and provides a final word of encouragement to the user.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Constraints:</span></p>
<ul id="S8.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S8.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Your output should be constructive, not condescending.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Provide code examples for fixes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S8.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If the code is exemplary, praise its strengths and highlight which conventions it follows well.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">8.7 </span>Proof: Variance of Weighted vs. Unweighted Average</h3>

<div id="S8.Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S8.Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 8.1</span></span><span id="S8.Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S8.Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If we have a sample of <math id="S8.Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> observations, <math id="S8.Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i},i\in 1,...\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i},i\in 1,...\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, each with standard deviation <math id="S8.Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and we have <math id="S8.Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\varphi}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\varphi}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> inversely proportional to variance of the corresponding <math id="S8.Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, then:</span></p>
<table id="S8.EGx47" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex64"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex64.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Var\left(\frac{1}{\rho}\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ρ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Var\left(\frac{1}{\rho}\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex64.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq Var\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}\bar{\varphi}_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\bar{\varphi}_{i}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq Var\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}\bar{\varphi}_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\bar{\varphi}_{i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS7.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S8.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have:</span></p>
<table id="S8.EGx48" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex65"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex65.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Var\left(\frac{1}{\rho}\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>ρ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Var\left(\frac{1}{\rho}\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex65.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\rho^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\sigma_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{\rho^{2}}\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\sigma_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S8.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">And:</span></p>
<table id="S8.EGx49" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S8.Ex66"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex66.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Var\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}\bar{\varphi}_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\bar{\varphi}_{i}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Var\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}\bar{\varphi}_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\bar{\varphi}_{i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex66.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=Var\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}(k/\sigma_{i}^{2})}{\sum_{j=1}^{\rho}(k/\sigma_{j}^{2})}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>ρ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Var\left(\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{\rho}\tau_{i}(k/\sigma_{i}^{2})}{\sum_{j=1}^{\rho}(k/\sigma_{j}^{2})}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S8.Ex66.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\because\bar{\varphi}_{i}=\frac{k}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}\text{for some constant $k$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∵</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>φ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>k</mi><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mtext>for some constant </mtext><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\because\bar{\varphi}_{i}=\frac{k}{\sigma_{i}^{2}}\text{for some constant $k$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
